# 2. Understanding the code by sections

Your firmware has five main functional sections.

| Section | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| Hardware configuration | Define motor, encoder and safety pins |
| Serial communication | Receive commands and transmit feedback |
| Encoder measurement | Determine wheel rotation and speed |
| Motor control | Calculate PWM using feedback |
| Main execution | Schedule control and communication |

Let's examine each.

## Section A — Hardware configuration

```cpp
constexpr uint8_t kRightPwmPin = 5;
constexpr uint8_t kRightIn1Pin = 7;
constexpr uint8_t kRightIn2Pin = 8;
```

These three pins control the right motor through the L293D.

- **PWM (5):** Controls the effective voltage applied to the motor through duty-cycle modulation.
- **IN1 (7):** Motor direction input.
- **IN2 (8):** Motor direction input.

The left motor uses pins 6, 9 and 10.

### Why `constexpr`?

```cpp
constexpr uint8_t kRightPwmPin = 5;
```

`constexpr` declares a compile-time constant.

`uint8_t` means an unsigned 8-bit integer, suitable for pin numbers.

Unlike a normal variable, this value cannot change during execution.

### Motor and encoder polarity

```cpp
constexpr int8_t kRightMotorSign = -1;
constexpr int8_t kLeftMotorSign = 1;

constexpr int8_t kRightEncoderSign = -1;
constexpr int8_t kLeftEncoderSign = 1;
```

These are important because the motors are mounted on opposite sides of the robot.

For example, applying the same electrical rotation direction to two mirrored motors might make one wheel move forward and the other backward.

The motor signs correct physical rotation direction.

The encoder signs independently correct measured rotation direction.

**Our goal:** When the robot moves forward, both measured wheel velocities should be positive.

