# 6. Motor actuation — `setMotor()`

```cpp
void setMotor(
    uint8_t pwm_pin,
    uint8_t in1_pin,
    uint8_t in2_pin,
    int8_t motor_sign,
    int pwm)
```

This function converts the controller output into electrical signals for the L293D.

### Direction control

```cpp
if (signed_pwm > 0) {
    digitalWrite(in1_pin, HIGH);
    digitalWrite(in2_pin, LOW);
}
```

Positive PWM selects one rotation direction.

Negative PWM selects the opposite direction.

Zero PWM disables motor drive.

### Speed control

```cpp
analogWrite(pwm_pin, abs(signed_pwm));
```

Arduino PWM values range from 0 to 255.

Your code currently limits the magnitude to 200.

For example:

\[
\text{Duty cycle}=\frac{200}{255}\times100
\]

\[
\boxed{78.4\%}
\]

At maximum allowed PWM, the enable signal has approximately a 78.4% duty cycle.

**Important:** Limiting PWM does not directly limit motor current. The L293D can still experience excessive current during startup or a stalled motor.

