# 9. Timing and safety

Your code has three important timing parameters.

| Parameter | Value | Purpose |
|---|---:|---|
| `kControlPeriodUs` | 10,000 µs | Motor control every 10 ms |
| `kTelemetryPeriodMs` | 20 ms | Encoder telemetry every 20 ms |
| `kCommandTimeoutMs` | 250 ms | Stop driving if commands are missing |

### Function: `loop()`

```cpp
void loop()
{
  readSerial();

  const unsigned long now_us = micros();

  if (now_us - last_control_us >= kControlPeriodUs) {
    updateControl(now_us);
  }

  const unsigned long now_ms = millis();

  if (now_ms - last_telemetry_ms >= kTelemetryPeriodMs) {
    last_telemetry_ms = now_ms;
    sendTelemetry();
  }
}
```

Unlike a program that uses `delay()`, this loop continuously checks elapsed time.

That allows serial communication, motor control and telemetry to operate without intentionally blocking one another.

### Watchdog behavior

Suppose the Raspberry Pi loses communication.

After 250 ms without a valid command:

```cpp
left_target_rad_s = 0.0F;
right_target_rad_s = 0.0F;
```

The PI function then returns zero PWM.

This removes motor drive, but it does **not** actively brake the wheels.

Also note:

```cpp
constexpr bool kUseEstop = false;
```

Your physical emergency-stop input is currently disabled in firmware. A software watchdog should not replace a hardware motor-power cutoff.

